We are given that $A_k = {1000 \choose k}(0.2)^k.$ To find the value of $k$ for which $A_k$ is the largest, we need to find the maximum of the function $f(k) = {1000 \choose k}(0.2)^k$ for $k = 0,1,2,\ldots,1000.$

We can rewrite $f(k)$ as follows:
\begin{align*}
f(k) &= \frac{1000!}{k!(1000-k)!}(0.2)^k \\
&= \frac{1000!}{k!(1000-k)!}(0.2)^k \\
&= \frac{1000 \cdot 999 \cdot 998 \cdot \cdots \cdot (1000-k+1)}{k!}(0.2)^k \\
&= \left(\frac{1000 \cdot 999 \cdot 998 \cdot \cdots \cdot (1000-k+1)}{k!}\right)(0.2)^k.
\end{align*}

To find the maximum of $f(k),$ we can take the derivative of $f(k)$ with respect to $k$ and set it equal to zero:
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dk}f(k) &= \frac{d}{dk}\left(\left(\frac{1000 \cdot 999 \cdot 998 \cdot \cdots \cdot (1000-k+1)}{k!}\right)(0.2)^k\right) \\
&= \left(\frac{1000 \cdot 999 \cdot 998 \cdot \cdots \cdot (1000-k+1)}{k!}\right)\left((0.2)^k \ln(0.2) + (0.2)^k \right) \\
&= \left(\frac{1000 \cdot 999 \cdot 998 \cdot \cdots \cdot (1000-k+1)}{k!}\right)(0.2)^k \ln(0.2) + \left(\frac{1000 \cdot 999 \cdot 998 \cdot \cdots \cdot (1000-k+1)}{k!}\right)(0.2)^k \\
&= \left(\frac{1000 \cdot 999 \cdot 998 \cdot \cdots \cdot (1000-k+1)}{k!}\right)(0.2)^k (\ln(0.2) + 1).
\end{align*}

Setting the derivative equal to zero, we get:
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{1000 \cdot 999 \cdot 998 \cdot \cdots \cdot (1000-k+1)}{k!}\right)(0.2)^k (\ln(0.2) + 1) &= 0.
\end{align*}

Since $\ln(0.2) + 1 \neq 0,$ we have:
\begin{align*}
\frac{1000 \cdot 999 \cdot 998 \cdot \cdots \cdot (1000-k+1)}{k!} &= 0.
\end{align*}

Therefore, the only solution is $k = 1000,$ which means that $A_{1000}$ is the largest. The answer is: 1000
